Integrate the combat-strength evaluator into the enemy AI's retreat and regroup decisions.

An enemy AI group should retreat when it is meaningfully weaker than its current opponent. Continue
reassessing relative strength while the group is attacking, retreating, and regrouping so fresh
observations can reverse an earlier decision. Casualties, damage, newly seen opponents, lost vision,
and reinforcements must all be able to trigger another judgment. Do not permanently latch a retreat or
attack decision.

Prevent rapid attack-retreat oscillation with named, configurable margins, hysteresis, and a minimum
decision interval where appropriate. A large or urgent change in observed strength should still be
able to force timely reconsideration. Treat uncertain or stale intelligence conservatively and
document the exact fallback behavior. Preserve existing pathfinding, safe retreat destinations,
formation behavior, and regroup mechanics.

Add deterministic tests covering:

- A weaker group deciding to retreat
- A stronger group deciding to attack or remain engaged
- An evenly matched or uncertain situation
- A retreating group receiving reinforcements and re-engaging
- An attacking group discovering reinforcements for the opponent and retreating
- Casualties changing the decision in either direction
- Lost vision and stale intelligence
- Hysteresis preventing oscillation near a threshold
- Periodic reassessment when no explicit observation event fires

After implementation:

1. Run the focused state-transition tests and the complete automated test suite.
2. Run the project's syntax/compilation checks.
3. Run fixed-seed headless scenarios that exercise retreat, regroup, and re-engagement.
4. Report the resulting decision precedence, thresholds, and state transitions.
5. If any screenshot is taken, save it only under `/work/screenshots`, inspect it immediately with
   `view_image`, and report every visual problem.

Do not yet allow strength to override the existing casualty-triggered retreat mechanic. Do not commit
changes.
